.TH AXSOCK 7 "20 August 2026" Linux "Linux Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
axsock \- AX.25 sockets served from userspace by libax25
.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
The AX.25 stack left the Linux kernel in 7.1.  Programs written against
.B libax25
still expect
.BI socket( AF_AX25 ", \fR\&...)"
to work.  Where the kernel does not answer any more \-\- or never did, as on
macOS and BSD \-\-
.B libax25
answers instead: it defines the socket calls itself and serves AX.25 from an
AGWPE server
.RB ( direwolf ,
or
.BR ax25netd (8)
in front of one) or from a
.B WAMPES
node.  Everything that is not AX.25 falls through to the real call.
.LP
This page is about the interception itself \-\- which calls it answers, how to
get it in front of a program that was not built for it, what it cannot reach,
and which environment variables steer it.  Which backend serves which port is
a different question, and it is settled per port, at
.BR bind (2),
out of the configuration rather than out of a probe:
.BR axports (5)
names the ports, the ones whose callsign is the AX.25 address of an interface
that is up are the kernel's, and of the rest the ones named in
.BR wampes.conf (5)
belong to a node while the AGWPE backend takes those it recognises as an
upstream name.  The AGWPE backend recognises an upstream by the name
.BR ax25netd_agwpe.conf (5)
gives it, or, where that file does not have it, by the port table the server
reports over the connection described below.  An entry of
.B axports
that no backend claims stays with the kernel, which refuses it in its own
words if it has no such port.
.LP
The AGWPE backend keeps one connection to a server, and
.B AXSOCK_HOST
decides which transport it uses: a value beginning with
.B /
is a unix socket and is used as given, anything else is a TCP host.  The
socket file, or the TCP port through
.BR AXSOCK_PORT ,
is the one thing that is not decided here, and that is deliberate - it is
read from
.BR ax25common.conf (5),
the same file the daemon reads for its own listener, so the backend
cannot end up at an endpoint
.BR ax25netd (8)
does not serve.  The loop port is a unix socket by default,
.IR /var/run/ax25/sockets/ax25netd.sock ,
and
.B loop socket no
together with
.B loop tcp
is how a station says it is a TCP port instead.
.LP
Set
.B AXSOCK_HOST
to name a radio program directly - direwolf listens on 8000 - and the
daemon is skipped entirely.
.LP
It is built when
.B configure
is given
.BR \-\-enable\-userspace\-ax25 ,
which happens by itself where there is no kernel AX.25 stack at all (macOS,
BSD) and has to be asked for on Linux.  A
.B libax25
built without it contains none of this, and preloading it does nothing.
.SH "THE CALLS IT ANSWERS"
.LP
.BR socket (2),
.BR bind (2),
.BR connect (2),
.BR listen (2),
.BR accept (2),
.BR send (2),
.BR sendto (2),
.BR write (2),
.BR recv (2),
.BR recvfrom (2),
.BR shutdown (2),
.BR close (2),
.BR setsockopt (2),
.BR getsockopt (2),
.BR ioctl (2),
.BR getsockname (2)
and
.BR getpeername (2)
are answered by the library.  On ELF they are defined under those very names,
as ordinary strong symbols \-\- not weak ones, not through a
.BR dlsym (3)
trampoline the application has to opt into.  On macOS they carry names of
their own and are put in front of libSystem by interposing, for the reason
given under PRELOADING; a caller sees no difference.
.LP
Each of them looks the descriptor up in a table of its own and hands
everything it does not know to the real call, so a program can serve telnet
over IPv4 or IPv6 and AX.25 side by side in one process, which is exactly
what a mailbox does.  The lookup is guarded by a counter that is zero until
the first AX.25 socket exists, so a process that never opens one pays a load
and a branch.
.LP
.BR read (2)
is deliberately not among them.  The descriptor the application holds is
readable by itself \-\- the read end of a pipe with the AGWPE backend, the
connection itself with WAMPES \-\- so
.BR read (2),
.BR poll (2),
.BR select (2)
and
.BR fcntl (2)
reach the kernel with nothing in between.
.SH PRELOADING
.LP
A program
.B linked
against
.B libax25
needs nothing beyond the configuration files.  A program that opens an AX.25
socket
.B without
ever calling a
.B libax25
function \-\- a script, or a daemon written when the kernel still answered \--
can be made to use this without being recompiled, provided the library is
loaded before the C library.  On Linux, with the directory this build was
configured to install the library into:
.PP
.RS
.nf
LD_PRELOAD=@@@AX25_LIBDIR@@@/libax25.so.0 program
.fi
.RE
.LP
The path is the one the library actually has, not a name for the dynamic
loader to resolve, so it has to be the one
.BR "ldconfig \-p" (8)
reports; a prefix other than the default, or a separate
.B \-\-libdir
at build time, moves it.
ELF has a flat namespace: the first definition of
.BR socket (2)
in the search order is the one every reference binds to, wherever that
reference comes from, so getting the library loaded first is the whole trick.
.SS "macOS, and why it is not the same trick"
.LP
Mach-O binds two-level.  Every import remembers which library it came from,
so a library inserted with
.B DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES
is loaded and then asked nothing.
.B DYLD_FORCE_FLAT_NAMESPACE
used to be the answer to that and is not honoured any more \-\- measured on
macOS 15.7.9: with the variable and without it, with the executable built the
ordinary way and with
.BR \-Wl,\-no_fixup_chains ,
and whether the inserted library is built flat or two-level, the call still
went to
.IR libSystem .
.LP
So on macOS the library carries an
.B interposing table
instead \-\- a
.B __DATA,__interpose
section naming pairs of functions, which dyld applies to every image in the
process.  Nothing has to be said on the command line beyond the insertion
itself:
.PP
.RS
.nf
DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES=/usr/local/lib/libax25.0.dylib program
.fi
.RE
.LP
Interposing brings one rule with it, and that rule shapes the library: dyld
leaves the bindings of the image that
.I provides
the interposition alone and rewrites everyone else\(cqs \-\- including what
.BR dlsym (3)
hands back, on any handle.  So on macOS the entry points do not carry the
libc names (they are
.IR axsock_ep_socket ", " axsock_ep_bind ", \&...),"
because the table has to be able to name libSystem\(cqs, and the fall-through
to the real call is a plain call rather than a
.BR dlsym (3)
pointer, because a pointer fetched that way comes back as the library\(cqs own
function.  That was measured before it was written down: a
.BR dlsym (3)
fall-through recurses until the stack is gone.
.LP
Two consequences worth knowing:
.TP 3
\(bu
a
.B libax25
linked
.I statically
into a program is not intercepted on macOS \-\- an interpose section in the
main executable is ignored.  Nothing in this suite links it statically.
.TP 3
\(bu
the libc names are no longer exported, so a program linked against an older
.B libax25
on macOS must be relinked: it asks for
.I _socket
in this library and no longer finds it.  Build and install
.BR libax25 ,
ax25-apps and ax25-tools together.
.SH "WHAT PRELOADING CANNOT REACH"
.LP
On either system:
.TP 3
\(bu
setuid and setgid programs.  The loader drops the variables in secure
execution mode.  Run them as root without the setuid bit instead.
.TP 3
\(bu
statically linked programs \-\- there is nothing to override.
.TP 3
\(bu
a program calling
.BI syscall( SYS_socket ", \fR\&...)"
directly.
.TP 3
\(bu
on macOS additionally anything protected by SIP, which strips every
.B DYLD_*
variable from the environment of such a process, and therefore from the
environment of everything it starts.
.SH "CHILDREN, AND THE DESCRIPTOR THEY INHERIT"
.LP
.BR ax25d (8)
accepts a call and hands it to a program on descriptor 0.  The program then
asks
.BI getpeername( 0 )
who is calling \-\-
.BR axspawn (8)
does exactly that before it picks a unix account, and so do
.BR rsuplnk (8)
and
.BR ttylinkd (8).
Where the connection is not a kernel socket there is nothing to ask: over a
socketpair the kernel answers AF_UNIX, and
.B axspawn
refuses the call rather than inventing a callsign.
.LP
So the parent says who is there, in one variable, and
.B libax25
picks it up when it loads, registers the descriptor with both addresses and
answers for it.  No child has to be changed.
.PP
.RS
.nf
AXSOCK_INHERIT=<fd> <local callsign> <peer callsign>
.fi
.RE
.LP
It is removed from the environment once read, because what is true of this
process is not true of anything it starts in turn.
.LP
.BR ax25d (8)
builds the environment of a service rather than passing its own \-\- a program
run for a caller on the air has no business inheriting what the daemon was
started with.  One exception, and it is necessary: the variable that says
.I which library we are
is passed on when it is set, or a station running the shim by preloading it
would lose it at exactly this edge and the child would find no AX.25 at all.
Only the names of the platform the daemon was built for \-\-
.B LD_PRELOAD
and
.BR LD_LIBRARY_PATH ,
or
.B DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES
and
.B DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH
on macOS \-\- since the other set would be noise in the environment of every
service.
.SH "MONITOR SOCKETS"
.LP
.BR listen (1),
.BR mheardd (8)
and
.BR net2kiss (8)
watch raw frames through a packet socket \-\-
.BI socket( PF_PACKET ", SOCK_PACKET, \fR\&...)",
which
.BR net2kiss (8)
still spells with
.BR AF_INET ,
as one did before Linux 2.2.  Both mean the same thing here, and what they
get depends on the backend:
.TP 8
kernel
the packet socket is real and the OS delivers the frames.
.TP 8
AGWPE
the server's raw monitor is switched on with the first such socket and off
again with the last, and every frame it hears arrives.  The AGWPE server
.RB ( ax25netd (8)
or
.BR direwolf )
has to be reachable, or
.BR socket (2)
fails unless a WAMPES node is configured, in which case the socket is handed
out and stays quiet instead.
.TP 8
WAMPES
works through the same monitor.  The WAMPES service protocol has no monitor
stream of its own, so the libax25 side pushes the frames it carries \-\- what
it receives from the node and what it sends to it \-\- into the AGWPE server's
raw monitor as
.B K
records, where a packet socket picks them up like any AGWPE frame, with the
digipeaters that have already repeated a frame marked
.BR * .
The frames are UI frames, and only UI frames: a node keeps AX.25 level 2 to
itself, so a connection it carries is traced on its own console and not here.
An AGWPE upstream is not limited this way \-\- it hands over the frames it
hears raw, link establishment included.
So an AGWPE server
.RB ( ax25netd (8))
has to be reachable here too.  When none is, a packet socket is handed out
all the same and stays quiet rather than failing, because a program that
opens a monitor beside its real work should lose one feature and not its
whole run; a line on standard error says so under
.BR AXSOCK_DEBUG .
.LP
A packet socket is not an AX.25 socket and has no
.BR bind (2)
that could name a port, so this is the one place where the per-port choice
above does not apply: a monitor follows the backend of the whole program, and
a program that has a kernel AX.25 stack monitors the kernel and sees only
what the kernel carries.  The frames served from userspace were never given
to the kernel and are not in that stream.  Set
.B AXSOCK_BACKEND
to
.B agwpe
to monitor the AGWPE feed instead, and the kernel's own frames are then the
ones that go unseen.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AXSOCK_BACKEND
.BR kernel ,
.B agwpe
or
.BR wampes ,
forcing one backend for the whole process.  It is an override and not the
normal way: without it the choice falls per port at
.BR bind (2),
out of the configuration as described above, and what is left over \-\- a port
no backend claims, and every monitor socket \-\- is decided once per process,
a single probe asking whether the kernel answers AF_AX25 and taking it if it
does, AGWPE if it does not.  See
.BR axports (5).
.TP
.B AXSOCK_DEBUG
Set to anything, and the library reports on standard error what it does with
each descriptor, including the whole conversation with a WAMPES node.  This
is the first thing to reach for.
.TP
.BR AXSOCK_HOST ", " AXSOCK_PORT
Environment variables: where the AGWPE backend looks for its server.
.B AXSOCK_HOST
decides the transport - a leading
.B /
is a path, anything else is a TCP host - and with it unset the backend
falls back on
.BR ax25common.conf (5),
so by default it finds the loop port of an
.BR ax25netd (8)
multiplexing the radios its
.BR ax25netd_agwpe.conf (5)
lists: a unix socket,
.IR /var/run/ax25/sockets/ax25netd.sock .
.BR AXSOCK_PORT
then gives the TCP port, 8200 where the file does not say.  To skip the
daemon and speak to a radio program directly, name it:
.B AXSOCK_HOST=127.0.0.1 AXSOCK_PORT=8000
for direwolf.  A value of
.B AXSOCK_HOST
that is a path selects the server's unix socket instead, which is gated by its
file permissions \-\- an
.BR ax25netd (8)
configured with
.B socket
and
.B "group hams"
is reachable by members of that group and nobody else.
.TP
.BR AXSOCK_USER ", " AXSOCK_PASSWORD
Presented to an AGWPE server that asks for a login \-\-
.BR ax25netd (8)
started with
.BR auth ,
where they are checked.
.B direwolf
ignores the login frame without a word, so against direwolf they are
decoration and the access rule is whoever can reach its port.
.TP
.B AXSOCK_INHERIT
Set by
.BR ax25d (8)
for the program it starts, as described above.  Not for hand use.
.TP
.B WAMPES_SOCKET
Overrides where every WAMPES node is expected to listen, ignoring
.BR wampes.conf (5).
Meant for trying something out rather than for running a station.
.SH "TRYING IT OUT"
.LP
.B axprobe
is built beside the library and never installed.  It is the one program here
that is
.B not
linked against
.BR libax25 :
it calls
.BR socket (2),
.BR bind (2),
.BR connect (2),
.BR listen (2)
and
.BR accept (2)
itself and reads nothing but
.BR axports (5).
Everything in ax25-apps and ax25-tools would find AX.25 either way, so none
of it can show whether preloading reaches anything.
.PP
.RS
.nf
LD_PRELOAD=@@@AX25_LIBDIR@@@/libax25.so.0 \\
    ./axprobe connect wampes:hfb DL1TST-1 DL1AAA DB0BBB
LD_PRELOAD=@@@AX25_LIBDIR@@@/libax25.so.0 ./axprobe listen agwpe DL9SAU-13
.fi
.RE
.LP
A source callsign of
.B \-
means the callsign of the
.BR axports (5)
entry, as when no
.B \-s
is given to
.BR call (1).
.B \-d
reaches the calls through
.BR dlsym (3)
instead of calling them straight, and says for each one which library
answered \-\- the mode in which an inserted library is seen on macOS at all,
and the difference between the two modes is itself the measurement.
.SH BUGS
.LP
A preloaded program calls nothing of the library on its own, and the port
table used to depend on that: it is filled by
.BR ax25_config_load_ports (),
which every program in the suite calls at startup.  With an empty table
.BR bind (2)
could not name the port \-\- the callsign in the first digipeater slot had
nothing to be looked up in \-\- so a WAMPES port was not recognised as one and
the socket went to AGWPE without a word.  It then righted itself on the next
.BR bind (2),
because the AGWPE path loads the table as a side effect, which made the
backend depend on the order of the binds.
.I port_of_bind()
in
.I wampes.c
now loads the table itself when it finds none, and both binds land where they
belong; it is written down here because a station running an older library
will meet it.
.SH FILES
.LP
@@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/axports
.br
@@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25netd_agwpe.conf
.br
@@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/wampes.conf
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR call (1),
.BR listen (1),
.BR ax25 (3),
.BR ax25 (4),
.BR axports (5),
.BR ax25common.conf (5),
.BR ax25netd_agwpe.conf (5),
.BR wampes.conf (5),
.BR ax25d (8),
.BR ax25netd (8),
.BR axspawn (8).
.LP
The WAMPES service protocol \-\- the grammar the
.B wampes
backend speaks, and what a leaner stack would have to offer to take its place
\-\- is written down in the WAMPES source, in
.IR doc/LIBAX25\-BRIDGE.md .
